If by Rudyard Kipling

If you can keep your head when all about you
Are losing theirs and blaming it on you,
If you can trust yourself when all men doubt you,
But make allowance for their doubting too;

If you can fill the unforgiving minute
With sixty seconds’ worth of distance run,
- that every minute there is,
- crowded with activity,
- ought to be called a minute wasted;

- who is he who shall deny it?
- who is he that defies it?

If you can dream -- and not make dreams your master;
- if you can think -- and not make thoughts your guide;
- if you can meet with Triumph and Disaster
- and treat those two impostors just the same;

- if you can bear to feel -- that although
- you have not succeeded,
- your heart is firm,
- and bricks have been your building material;
- you have no patterning of false humility,
- and you know only who you are;

- if you can be happy -- and sad in what you do;
- if you can meet with Triumph and Disaster
- and treat those two impostors just the same;

- if you can talk with Parliaments and Party
- and not feel you're in a minority,
- if you can be all things to all people
- and yet keep your self-identity whole;

- if you can work with your hands and your brains
- and make your work your play and play your work;
- if you can bear to see your children's futures
- and not be ashamed of their futures,
- if you can meet with Triumph and Disaster
- and treat those two impostors just the same;

